misconceptions = ["The model itself executes the code or API calls it requests."]
+++

When an application describes some tools in the prompt, such as a name, a
purpose and the arguments each takes, a model trained for it can respond
with a structured call instead of prose: in effect, "call `search` with
these arguments".

The model executes nothing. The application around it parses the request,
decides whether it is allowed, runs it, and appends the result to the
context as more tokens. Then generation continues, now with the result in
view. Permissions, sandboxing and logging all live in that application,
never in the model.

An *agent* is this loop repeated: generate, call a tool, read the result,
generate again, until the task is done. [Retrieval](#rag) is often just one
of the tools, alongside running code, reading files or calling an API.

How tools are described to the model and connected to the application varies
between vendors. [MCP](#mcp) is one attempt to standardise the connection
side.
